    {
      "name": "mergeContinuousPaths",
      "overloads": [
        {
          "signature": "mergeContinuousPaths(Tree, double) -> int",
          "params": [
            {
              "name": "tree",
              "type": "Tree",
              "java_type": "Tree",
              "description": "- maximum angle (in degrees) between parent end tangent and child start tangent for paths to be considered continuous. Suggested values:\n\n15-20°: strict, only nearly straight continuations 30°: moderate (default), allows gentle curves 45°: permissive, may merge actual branches\n\nUse 0 or negative to disable merging.",
              "javadoc_name": "angleThreshold"
            },
            {
              "name": "angleThreshold",
              "type": "float",
              "java_type": "double",
              "description": "",
              "javadoc_name": "param_1"
            }
          ],
          "return_type": "int",
          "java_return_type": "int",
          "return_description": "the number of paths that were merged (and removed from the tree)",
          "throws": {}
        }
      ],
      "documentation": "Java method: mergeContinuousPaths",
      "javadoc_description": "Merges paths that continue along the same trajectory at branch points, reducing fragmentation in auto-traced reconstructions.\n\n**Problem:** Auto-tracers like `GWDTTracer` often create separate paths at every branch point, even when the neurite continues straight through. This may fragment continuous paths into smaller segments.\n\n**Solution:** At each junction where a parent path ends and children begin, this method checks if any child's trajectory aligns with the parent's. If the angle between their direction vectors is below the threshold, the most aligned child is merged into the parent, creating a longer continuous path.\n\n**Algorithm:**\n\n#. For each path with children, compute the parent's end tangent vector (direction at its terminal segment)\n\n#. For each child, compute its start tangent vector\n\n#. Find the child with the smallest angle to the parent direction\n\n#. If this angle ≤ threshold: append child's nodes to parent, reparent grandchildren to the extended parent, remove child from tree\n\n#. Repeat until no more merges are possible\n\n**Criteria for merging:** Two paths are merged when:\n\n- The child starts at the parent's endpoint (branch point)\n\n- The angle between their direction vectors ≤ `angleThreshold`\n\n- The child is the *most aligned* among all siblings\n\n**Example:** A traced axon might be split into 5 paths at 4 branch points: \n```\nBefore: P1 → P2 → P3 → P4 → P5  (with side branches B1, B2, B3, B4)\n After:  P1 (merged trunk) with children B1, B2, B3, B4\n```\n",
      "category": "I/O Operations",
      "deprecated": false,
      "since_version": null,
      "see_also": [],
      "examples": [
        "Before: P1 → P2 → P3 → P4 → P5  (with side branches B1, B2, B3, B4)\n After:  P1 (merged trunk) with children B1, B2, B3, B4"
      ]
    },
